  'Religious Ethics examines how religious traditions form moral persons and communities, interpret authoritative sources, reason about virtue, law, justice, care, violence, economics, bodies, technology, and public life, and deliberate across deep pluralism. Students learn to read primary sources, compare moral vocabularies carefully, analyze contested cases, and write arguments that are historically grounded, ethically rigorous, and publicly intelligible.',
  $rethjson${"slug": "religious-ethics", "title": "Religious Ethics", "description": "Religious Ethics examines how religious traditions form moral persons and communities, interpret authoritative sources, reason about virtue, law, justice, care, violence, economics, bodies, technology, and public life, and deliberate across deep pluralism. Students learn to read primary sources, compare moral vocabularies carefully, analyze contested cases, and write arguments that are historically grounded, ethically rigorous, and publicly intelligible.", "topics": [{"id": "018773eb-609e-48ab-beee-5366974e0f24", "type": "topic", "title": "Religious Ethics as an Academic Field", "description": "This opening section defines religious ethics as a comparative, historical, textual, philosophical, and lived field of study. The point is to see ethical claims as embedded in communities, practices, authorities, and disputes rather than as isolated opinions.", "children": [{"id": "7019ddbe-8ba8-4420-b59b-e4722f86dde5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Defining Religious Ethics", "children": [{"id": "5741949d-a769-4411-bc7a-1611f6e3669d", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral norms, virtues, practices, and ultimate goods"}, {"id": "a35e2700-8c7e-4404-8227-b773d25908a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive, interpretive, normative, and constructive approaches"}, {"id": "ea8c051b-0bbf-4348-aaec-86e7be4764f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Insider and outsider vocabularies"}, {"id": "31172b64-5953-4c73-b40c-0c43b94b6ca1", "type": "concept", "title": "Tradition, change, and moral disagreement"}], "description": "Students learn what makes an ethical question religiously meaningful and how the field differs from theology, philosophy, anthropology, and professional applied ethics."}, {"id": "87dafb27-7b60-4834-99ea-56627f24a664", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Academic Study of Religion and Ethics", "children": [{"id": "b914c454-9736-46cf-ba81-55f66d95b5fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Myth, ritual, symbol, text, law, and community"}, {"id": "9cf543ba-7d11-42e9-859f-443f2891e462", "type": "concept", "title": "Lived religion and everyday moral practice"}, {"id": "ea2bf1ef-9630-493c-a416-a45efe987389", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical-critical and ethnographic evidence"}, {"id": "dfab163f-03d1-466d-81c9-5491f7c3d8c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding reductionism and romanticization"}], "description": "This subtopic grounds ethical analysis in the broader academic study of religion, including attention to power, context, comparison, and evidence."}, {"id": "5ec5304b-d148-4a7c-8b67-aff70a55ef97", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Moral Problems as Case Studies", "children": [{"id": "840e3298-937b-4730-9586-ec0b76fa4081", "type": "concept", "title": "Fact patterns and competing goods"}, {"id": "284c6fc8-2ae4-4989-85fa-5af1933402d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder mapping and moral salience"}, {"id": "e9f2f63b-6ea2-4d95-a71f-771b154773b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Norms, precedents, and practical wisdom"}, {"id": "69664a59-10d2-4fad-a79a-514c0df3d090", "type": "concept", "title": "Writing a defensible ethical conclusion"}], "description": "Students practice turning real ethical conflicts into analyzable cases with facts, stakeholders, values, traditions, and possible judgments."}]}, {"id": "857d3263-7cbb-46de-afad-db7c0985a12a", "type": "topic", "title": "Sources of Moral Authority in Religious Traditions", "description": "Religious ethics depends on what a community treats as authoritative. This section teaches students to identify sources of authority and evaluate how they are interpreted, ranked, contested, and revised.", "children": [{"id": "3cbfeedb-3e97-40f0-8103-4e10f90c485c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scripture, Revelation, and Sacred Narrative", "children": [{"id": "e63753f9-9b33-4bb6-91d5-164297de1cbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon, commentary, and translation"}, {"id": "bcdfd429-38f7-4042-9157-ea07c2381d4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative exemplars and moral imagination"}, {"id": "7e64c9ea-c4c0-4b11-996d-2ba52c101ba9", "type": "concept", "title": "Proof-texting versus contextual interpretation"}, {"id": "410747ce-3003-476a-84d7-67ae66f67ef8", "type": "concept", "title": "Disputes over literal, allegorical, and historical readings"}], "description": "Students examine how texts and stories authorize moral claims and how interpretation mediates between ancient sources and present decisions."}, {"id": "c17efef6-ff00-42b3-8793-096e7e4efd40", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Law, Command, Conscience, and Community", "children": [{"id": "7c89e5c8-af76-493b-b518-72333de55033", "type": "concept", "title": "Divine command and covenant obligations"}, {"id": "69090247-0e4f-4105-a3c7-12fc353ae564", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious law and jurisprudence"}, {"id": "0fbd5efd-a20f-4107-a684-fd821fb0f098", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscience, discernment, and moral agency"}, {"id": "d23a4322-21d5-45ac-9d41-ed44463ce838", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority of clergy, teachers, elders, and institutions"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how commands and laws function within communities and how conscience can affirm, challenge, or refine inherited norms."}, {"id": "5ac7a4ea-9d1f-4974-b230-beb20790e30a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reason, Experience, Tradition, and Practice", "children": [{"id": "61ea0c37-eae0-4ff9-8538-2327e3273a59", "type": "concept", "title": "Natural law and practical reason"}, {"id": "0de025eb-3d86-4b88-9ef4-eb11cdcf2949", "type": "concept", "title": "Experience, testimony, and moral insight"}, {"id": "6ab3415e-030a-4e1e-a87c-d6fbaa2df7b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Custom, precedent, and embodied practice"}, {"id": "4e8df1a3-989e-4299-864a-230320362ce6", "type": "concept", "title": "Tradition as continuity and argument"}], "description": "Students explore sources that are not reducible to scripture, including philosophical reason, spiritual experience, communal practice, and historical memory."}]}, {"id": "59f3fa28-e4a4-4904-84e3-068592b1dbe0", "type": "topic", "title": "Normative Ethical Frameworks and Moral Reasoning", "description": "This section gives students a portable toolkit for comparing moral arguments. The goal is not to force traditions into one framework, but to recognize recurring structures of reasoning.", "children": [{"id": "7cbe15a3-4a66-4364-8957-a8d527d33089", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Virtue, Character, and Moral Formation", "children": [{"id": "06d21690-7a4b-4942-9afc-aed94ca55331", "type": "concept", "title": "Virtues, vices, and moral exemplars"}, {"id": "0320f205-2ca0-4d29-9e25-757632dfefdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Habit, discipline, worship, and practice"}, {"id": "1efac465-fdae-4210-a72a-2c8ca4cf4674", "type": "concept", "title": "Character and community formation"}, {"id": "d5a9f270-a21d-4e2e-a347-d61175935b28", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical wisdom and moral perception"}], "description": "Virtue ethics asks what kind of person or community one should become and how habits, exemplars, and disciplines cultivate moral excellence."}, {"id": "b68e3a71-111f-4102-9b89-459f58a4f0b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Law, Duty, Command, and Obligation", "children": [{"id": "314c56b1-822d-46ff-b8e0-b8800a1b7380", "type": "concept", "title": "Deontology and moral rules"}, {"id": "278d34be-2559-490d-a9aa-3519cb2bf6a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Divine command and revealed law"}, {"id": "0cddf312-7c54-422d-abb7-f445b86cd97d", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights, duties, and role obligations"}, {"id": "9260cbea-6fed-4f4e-bd62-598fa4a38c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict between duties"}], "description": "Duty-centered reasoning highlights requirements, prohibitions, rights, promises, and obedience to divine or communal authority."}, {"id": "2f900ae3-dd36-48b0-a49c-d2cff227c848", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Consequences, Care, Liberation, and Justice", "children": [{"id": "daf5fb08-3a36-4f74-9690-d6e5384cd3d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Consequentialism and public goods"}, {"id": "f3624497-ee8e-4426-bf37-89630e5c607e", "type": "concept", "title": "Care ethics and relational responsibility"}, {"id": "186afca0-45e1-44bd-b1a6-ad94aba00030", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberationist and anti-oppressive ethics"}, {"id": "d07a6359-9fc7-47e6-b506-8c988140a176", "type": "concept", "title": "Distributive, procedural, and restorative justice"}], "description": "Students learn frameworks that judge actions by outcomes, relationships, oppression, repair, and the distribution of benefits and burdens."}]}, {"id": "96362db0-5e9a-4017-9f58-64b40ec6c346", "type": "topic", "title": "Scripture, Interpretation, and Moral Argument", "description": "Religious ethics often turns on how texts are read. This section trains students to analyze interpretive moves, historical contexts, and moral uses of scripture.", "children": [{"id": "0cc602d5-5f36-489b-a2bf-4e32e9e95cf4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hermeneutics and Context", "children": [{"id": "064b59d7-82f3-496a-ae06-c86727f12546", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre, narrative, poetry, law, and wisdom literature"}, {"id": "3360f1d7-d870-43c7-b306-bfe5e68c5949", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical setting and social world"}, {"id": "8c5f15dd-dc47-40ee-8a9d-cb5e44663a2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception history and commentary traditions"}, {"id": "50ee06d1-8748-459e-b42a-7f28ca23737f", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and semantic range"}], "description": "Students learn that ethical interpretation requires context: language, genre, history, audience, and the community of reception."}, {"id": "20217f02-1072-4a46-ace5-afe3258504e5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive Conflict", "children": [{"id": "4aa70e10-7da2-4f80-9888-4c19b2141a7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing canons of interpretation"}, {"id": "2103ba18-f73c-4b1e-b667-0f98e147687b", "type": "concept", "title": "Continuity, reform, and rupture"}, {"id": "7bc317d6-56a7-4822-af06-4ae60044f3a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority readings and contested authority"}, {"id": "4f9d7e6f-3fad-4936-9959-dc7b3704ae95", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical stakes of interpretation"}], "description": "Religious communities often disagree about the moral meaning of the same source. This subtopic teaches students to compare arguments fairly."}, {"id": "07ab6b02-3ae0-4537-83c4-110822053128", "type": "subtopic", "title": "From Text to Contemporary Judgment", "children": [{"id": "ce29e3c9-3213-472d-bf05-e9d4d4085098", "type": "concept", "title": "Analogy and precedent"}, {"id": "ce030554-583c-4db6-9aff-598df6462afa", "type": "concept", "title": "Principles versus rules"}, {"id": "b729d83d-908e-46ce-8008-7556ae29b5f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Casuistry and case comparison"}, {"id": "334c8b34-c593-4881-8f68-b4441049928c", "type": "concept", "title": "Public reasoning from particular traditions"}], "description": "Students practice moving from ancient or classical sources to modern cases without ignoring historical distance or present complexity."}]}, {"id": "8787d745-a70b-47aa-b286-9a34568e115d", "type": "topic", "title": "Ritual, Formation, Virtue, and Moral Community", "description": "This section treats ethics as formed through bodies, habits, rituals, institutions, and communal life. Students learn why moral knowledge is often practiced before it is abstracted.", "children": [{"id": "1681714c-0cd8-4098-ba3d-832209095191", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual and Habit", "children": [{"id": "ae65af4f-50e1-47f0-be3d-b285d0eeb294", "type": "concept", "title": "Prayer, fasting, pilgrimage, confession, and service"}, {"id": "d133deab-5e6f-4702-854d-e63451caba16", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodied discipline and moral attention"}, {"id": "17aec512-7f5a-40da-a221-13a985f4536d", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual failure, hypocrisy, and reform"}, {"id": "caf14e90-b758-4918-8182-e1354c55de19", "type": "concept", "title": "Communal rhythms and ethical memory"}], "description": "Ritual can train attention, desire, memory, gratitude, restraint, and responsibility. Students ask how repeated practice shapes moral perception."}, {"id": "f03c8324-2078-4944-9a8f-52b1a04771ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exemplars, Saints, Sages, and Prophets", "children": [{"id": "9d862bc7-62e3-4697-ae31-983d3999d32d", "type": "concept", "title": "Imitation and critique of exemplars"}, {"id": "3586f0cf-83a0-4242-b9aa-31fc2dd3eb81", "type": "concept", "title": "Prophetic witness and moral courage"}, {"id": "dda5e10f-2194-4f6b-bb76-e04cde97d594", "type": "concept", "title": "Martyrdom, sainthood, and renunciation"}, {"id": "29016378-d44e-4b60-95f2-b974023ab049", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of hero-centered ethics"}], "description": "Religious ethics often teaches through model lives. Students study exemplarity as a way of forming moral imagination."}, {"id": "e2f0169c-afb4-4165-a58a-2f67576bb8cd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Community, Identity, and Accountability", "children": [{"id": "eb875a7d-a052-4c2e-9d67-13c8b4bb433e", "type": "concept", "title": "Belonging, identity, and obligation"}, {"id": "d3cd8e77-87d3-4f71-b279-14494509866f", "type": "concept", "title": "Confession, repentance, forgiveness, and repair"}, {"id": "f55865a2-b7db-4244-b6ec-131a6612012d", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional ethics and abuse of authority"}, {"id": "510e0364-085c-4ccf-ba4c-5707d7e6b3d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Community boundaries and outsiders"}], "description": "Moral practice happens within groups that sustain, discipline, and sometimes distort ethical commitments."}]}, {"id": "8e268e16-65ff-4197-b2d8-f7c6084d2d70", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative Religious Ethics and Translation", "description": "Comparison is powerful but risky. This section teaches students to compare traditions with precision, humility, and awareness of historical and cultural differences.", "children": [{"id": "a073c81b-4719-4951-bf70-410533df08cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Methods of Comparison", "children": [{"id": "f63ee958-02dc-46e4-aab0-ab66f34cfe3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Functional, genealogical, and thematic comparison"}, {"id": "e1d5298f-d763-4498-afc7-9bcecac79e3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description and local categories"}, {"id": "920fd613-0739-49b4-927d-a5a836c187a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Asymmetry and incommensurability"}, {"id": "b5b17317-c6c6-48a6-9b2b-1eb31214f56b", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding ranking traditions as morally superior"}], "description": "Students learn how to compare ethical concepts without assuming that similar words mean identical things across traditions."}, {"id": "86965151-5b2b-431b-a3b8-8d64fc2d0c6b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation of Moral Concepts", "children": [{"id": "bd38fd88-1ca9-4166-b100-f05e6926ace9", "type": "concept", "title": "Semantic fields and untranslatable terms"}, {"id": "45215254-e12c-4707-83af-fbd4f0552532", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing dharma, Torah, Sharia, ren, agape, and karuna"}, {"id": "30ea6f93-34d3-439d-ba8e-0b7cda2dbcb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Public concepts and tradition-specific meanings"}, {"id": "ba6de4d8-4ec5-4bfe-8391-a04ba56bcf56", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation as interpretation"}], "description": "This subtopic asks what is gained and lost when terms such as justice, compassion, sin, duty, liberation, and dignity are translated."}, {"id": "deca383e-c878-413b-b861-54f4b309fd50", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical Dialogue and Pluralism", "children": [{"id": "afd3e6fc-b959-4955-ac20-2b1cee972722", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism, exclusivism, inclusivism, and relativism"}, {"id": "0e435449-402a-4f90-be7b-af517a44b3f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared cases and different moral languages"}, {"id": "7ccc2e88-768e-4460-b9f4-0c43fce0bc1b", "type": "concept", "title": "Respectful critique and accountable listening"}, {"id": "44ec2958-847f-4553-9145-502e1c7c795b", "type": "concept", "title": "Coalition ethics in public life"}], "description": "Students practice dialogue across disagreement without collapsing differences into easy consensus."}]}, {"id": "f1ccbf3e-7ae2-40ae-ac64-6a1b13af5b44", "type": "topic", "title": "Judaism, Law, Covenant, and Ethical Life", "description": "This section introduces Jewish ethical reasoning through covenant, law, interpretation, memory, communal obligation, and debate. Students see ethics as a living argument across texts and communities.", "children": [{"id": "95e86cbe-9bb8-422f-9449-a316f473470e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Torah, Halakhah, and Rabbinic Reasoning", "children": [{"id": "4477ac12-be6a-4954-98bf-b363a806e47b", "type": "concept", "title": "Mitzvot and commanded life"}, {"id": "ce037a7e-2583-470a-ba3f-7fdd96b22211", "type": "concept", "title": "Talmudic argument and legal reasoning"}, {"id": "36adb776-3a11-4885-b3c8-71c27fdfebdc", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsa literature and modern cases"}, {"id": "f70ddd32-5d25-42a2-9e3b-ad4e0a532916", "type": "concept", "title": "Law, narrative, and moral formation"}], "description": "Students examine how Jewish law and interpretation shape moral practice through commandments, commentary, and case-based reasoning."}, {"id": "fec9fb57-aeb7-486a-847b-8d5f313d4a91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Justice, Mercy, and Covenant Responsibility", "children": [{"id": "ab9188a3-c11d-4117-8870-9a64aa3c0eaf", "type": "concept", "title": "Tzedakah, mishpat, hesed, and tikkun olam"}, {"id": "8a2debef-0d47-4a5b-a979-8f909da9b4f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Stranger, widow, orphan, poor, and neighbor"}, {"id": "fbe0e6af-f0b9-4f6b-8355-c5cc7683cb0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sabbath, labor, debt, and rest"}, {"id": "ca8000c0-05c1-4d8d-b0ee-4c796e6437ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, suffering, and moral obligation"}], "description": "This subtopic highlights ethical themes of justice, care for the vulnerable, covenant fidelity, and communal responsibility."}, {"id": "53424088-0263-4af4-9955-37a7e66617f0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modern Jewish Ethical Debates", "children": [{"id": "5dad0923-b701-4236-b17d-d72a362114ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Bioethics and end-of-life decisions"}, {"id": "5b96a2ff-ec9b-45cc-b50a-a7dd7469cf7c", "type": "concept", "title": "War, peace, and state power"}, {"id": "da3d772f-9ecf-4739-9d01-c61cc161a9f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, sexuality, and family law"}, {"id": "919f0525-5ca5-4d53-b134-59a744823c30", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora, peoplehood, and public responsibility"}], "description": "Students apply Jewish sources to contemporary issues while attending to denominational diversity and historical context."}]}, {"id": "d1033d98-6e68-4ca5-815e-8a85a417252e", "type": "topic", "title": "Christian Ethics, Discipleship, and Social Teaching", "description": "This section explores Christian ethics through scripture, virtue, discipleship, church tradition, natural law, conscience, and social teaching. Students compare diverse Christian approaches rather than treating Christianity as one voice.", "children": [{"id": "5897b241-00fc-4504-9759-793f6128073d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scripture, Virtue, and Discipleship", "children": [{"id": "6692f304-b9ed-4d8e-bbf6-4c7abcbf9586", "type": "concept", "title": "Kingdom of God, love, grace, sin, and forgiveness"}, {"id": "34eca303-f214-4cc4-9c42-f5c3ef9e84e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Sermon on the Mount and enemy love"}, {"id": "fc7381fd-b06b-42a1-ad14-c01b1f940d04", "type": "concept", "title": "Virtues, practices, and sanctification"}, {"id": "ba48bc54-1ab8-4ea8-92d1-9d32110bd703", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral exemplars and saints"}], "description": "Students study how biblical narratives, Jesus traditions, Pauline letters, and virtue language shape Christian moral imagination."}, {"id": "1fac152d-4338-4a74-988b-3616fe4654ac", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Natural Law, Conscience, and Church Teaching", "children": [{"id": "4721b4a5-468e-48d6-99ee-d0285fc437f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Natural law and practical reason"}, {"id": "022e8d81-4b7f-450a-a166-a2bbba51869a", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscience, authority, and discernment"}, {"id": "e0e50a77-10ad-4736-8df9-d8b7eabf18ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Catholic social teaching and common good"}, {"id": "05dc5913-5551-4ce4-9457-ade466fedb35", "type": "concept", "title": "Reformation, liberation, feminist, and Black Christian ethics"}], "description": "This subtopic traces major frameworks for Christian moral reasoning across Catholic, Protestant, Orthodox, and liberationist contexts."}, {"id": "b8647de5-5d5e-4697-82fa-4ec5f2621291", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Christian Ethics in Public Life", "children": [{"id": "b4efab40-77c1-423d-bc49-68f1343cc9f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Just war and pacifist traditions"}, {"id": "101ec7e3-aa81-48b1-ab52-137d0fef77cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Social gospel, civil rights, and liberation movements"}, {"id": "650e3a36-1389-4382-80d6-99ad0940580e", "type": "concept", "title": "Family, gender, and sexual ethics"}, {"id": "2bb72424-5000-4f66-9ceb-d0d8b4a814b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Creation care and climate justice"}], "description": "Students analyze how Christian communities argue about democracy, poverty, sexuality, war, race, migration, and ecological responsibility."}]}, {"id": "b9ea3a17-62b9-4bf7-8ce3-033d3ce1e05b", "type": "topic", "title": "Islamic Ethics, Law, Virtue, and Public Life", "description": "This section introduces Islamic ethical reasoning through Qur'an, hadith, Sharia, jurisprudence, virtue, intention, communal welfare, and mercy. Students learn to distinguish law, ethics, spirituality, and politics.", "children": [{"id": "831c6f92-5846-40a0-bf84-8d3098dbe031", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Qur'an, Hadith, and Moral Formation", "children": [{"id": "0aeb507a-1835-4f46-a273-3df9b1360f6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Tawhid, taqwa, adl, rahma, and ihsan"}, {"id": "ab45f48a-b94d-421f-b7c4-1d7632750d3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Prophetic example and hadith ethics"}, {"id": "555e20ab-846d-4d56-a2f2-e10ba53789d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Five pillars as moral formation"}, {"id": "aef77368-3246-4273-b416-1a43f1a05335", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability, repentance, and mercy"}], "description": "Students study how Islamic sources frame moral life through worship, intention, accountability, justice, and character."}, {"id": "98efcd22-72c3-4136-a8cc-d22226659262", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sharia, Fiqh, Maqasid, and Public Welfare", "children": [{"id": "ec2b58f0-0522-4e28-a101-9bb13f23954a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sources of law and schools of jurisprudence"}, {"id": "acb5128f-adc5-43ae-966d-37704b369471", "type": "concept", "title": "Maqasid al-sharia and maslaha"}, {"id": "1ebc81e3-4235-4dd7-bb12-b092516a281a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fatwa, custom, and changing circumstances"}, {"id": "99765e4c-46c2-4688-90e7-0401d4e8ec40", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority jurisprudence and plural societies"}], "description": "This subtopic explains Islamic law as a tradition of reasoning, interpretation, and practical judgment."}, {"id": "f078a261-b56f-4b33-894f-ae4a16e23862", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Contemporary Muslim Ethical Debates", "children": [{"id": "9e508232-9b06-45d1-b440-95484d8bfebe", "type": "concept", "title": "Finance, charity, and economic justice"}, {"id": "00c60287-291e-4796-8b8d-88da3d1dd82e", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, family, and authority"}, {"id": "c33f8a46-62be-48c3-b702-33786f5d59b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Bioethics, medicine, and technology"}, {"id": "7828029f-b375-4a3f-9ae9-574cc3485981", "type": "concept", "title": "Human rights, citizenship, violence, and peace"}], "description": "Students examine contemporary debates with attention to diversity across regions, schools, movements, and lived Muslim communities."}]}, {"id": "14321468-e946-45c5-abaa-a5c3194b0d6b", "type": "topic", "title": "South Asian Religious Ethics: Hindu, Buddhist, Jain, and Sikh Traditions", "description": "This section introduces major South Asian moral vocabularies while emphasizing internal diversity and historical change. Students examine duty, liberation, compassion, nonviolence, caste, service, and social reform.", "children": [{"id": "83d0f39b-7a40-405b-9c13-4fb20075a41c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dharma, Karma, Liberation, and Social Order", "children": [{"id": "9b1a13a6-d6a9-44d3-b60b-cfc564c90655", "type": "concept", "title": "Dharma, svadharma, karma, samsara, and moksha"}, {"id": "398dff01-f6cf-47c1-a3b1-a6c7006f842e", "type": "concept", "title": "Householder, renouncer, and life-stage ethics"}, {"id": "fd94b5be-c528-4843-a208-d6a0e0df2b5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste, gender, and critique of social hierarchy"}, {"id": "9933b8cd-e48b-47fd-ae2f-9fe2ffdc0adc", "type": "concept", "title": "Bhagavad Gita and moral conflict"}], "description": "Students explore dharma and karma as moral frameworks that connect action, duty, consequence, cosmic order, and social location."}, {"id": "7666fe56-dfcb-4932-aef3-21f2bb5f2184", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Compassion, Nonviolence, and Renunciation", "children": [{"id": "51b434f7-8b3e-4c77-b605-0409c43a289d", "type": "concept", "title": "Four noble truths and eightfold path"}, {"id": "c6a8d0d8-0e87-4fc2-8638-d9d23ed3cbba", "type": "concept", "title": "Karuna, metta, bodhisattva ideals, and skillful means"}, {"id": "9e2a6b75-16ec-4067-8372-90852c650252", "type": "concept", "title": "Ahimsa, aparigraha, and Jain vows"}, {"id": "43760210-8323-4796-bf1d-ea2230a37b29", "type": "concept", "title": "Vegetarianism, animals, and ecological restraint"}], "description": "This subtopic compares Buddhist and Jain ethics through suffering, attachment, non-harm, discipline, and liberation."}, {"id": "7f77238a-50b9-4982-b82e-34030e910ee5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sikh Ethics, Seva, and Justice", "children": [{"id": "21ba3a79-0f62-4529-ac4a-cd2222ea9be9", "type": "concept", "title": "Naam, seva, kirat karo, and vand chhako"}, {"id": "bad7a538-1bf5-4359-88e5-f081539687a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Langar, equality, and anti-caste commitments"}, {"id": "a99ad5b9-bf3d-4639-9729-d1105fd76d88", "type": "concept", "title": "Miri-piri and public responsibility"}, {"id": "67f120ef-c0dd-47f7-9f9e-909a158cc720", "type": "concept", "title": "Justice, defense, and moral courage"}], "description": "Students examine Sikh moral life through devotion, equality, service, courage, and communal responsibility."}]}, {"id": "91bca19d-1ab2-424a-babf-effe683537a1", "type": "topic", "title": "East Asian Religious Ethics: Confucian, Daoist, Buddhist, and Shinto Contexts", "description": "This section explores East Asian ethical traditions through relational virtue, harmony, ritual, spontaneity, compassion, ancestors, and civic belonging. Students learn how moral life is shaped by family, state, nature, and cultivation.", "children": [{"id": "b0492c55-0723-4932-96d4-aecdab764512", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Confucian Relational Ethics", "children": [{"id": "24b806f6-17b4-4046-8cea-5f260bbd253f", "type": "concept", "title": "Ren, li, yi, xiao, and junzi"}, {"id": "04dcc2cd-2dc4-4f07-8da9-9809bb9aa4a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Family, hierarchy, reciprocity, and role ethics"}, {"id": "0098862c-5ae1-49d6-b79b-c94ba1ff859d", "type": "concept", "title": "Mencius, Xunzi, and moral cultivation"}, {"id": "d47bbe77-e85f-4931-ae57-8bbf28c4be55", "type": "concept", "title": "Merit, governance, and public responsibility"}], "description": "Students study Confucian ethics as cultivation of humane, trustworthy, ritually appropriate relationships."}, {"id": "86ed8ac6-a53f-4243-8088-45e51348c1d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Daoist Naturalness and Critique", "children": [{"id": "992eadd4-8aa6-4bd8-aeae-b93bf5f59bac", "type": "concept", "title": "Dao, de, wu-wei, ziran, and simplicity"}, {"id": "c8bf09ac-c924-42e3-bc20-2f2455b4e1ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique of artificial norms and ambition"}, {"id": "bd4df514-b2aa-4217-bd16-881c0d45d5d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Ecological resonance and non-domination"}, {"id": "51522869-a215-4e2e-b0a1-40e75a4bfe45", "type": "concept", "title": "Sages, spontaneity, and political restraint"}], "description": "Daoist ethics questions coercive moralism and emphasizes responsiveness, simplicity, humility, and alignment with the Dao."}, {"id": "985c0aeb-a76e-437d-a1ce-88829f3b5e54", "type": "subtopic", "title": "East Asian Buddhist and Shinto Moral Worlds", "children": [{"id": "31c34a9f-bba6-4e8a-ab38-585e42cd059c", "type": "concept", "title": "Bodhisattva ethics and compassion"}, {"id": "c92aab6b-c654-40fa-9219-032507c62434", "type": "concept", "title": "Zen practice and everyday discipline"}, {"id": "843e5a17-f1a0-4081-8540-5ce666a31652", "type": "concept", "title": "Kami, purity, place, and ritual responsibility"}, {"id": "78415dd8-529b-42b6-9ef7-f2919e6ffbc1", "type": "concept", "title": "Modern state, nationalism, and religious ethics"}], "description": "Students examine compassion, interdependence, purity, ancestors, place, and communal rites as ethical resources."}]}, {"id": "6753dd2c-3536-41b4-9d52-dd202ab7c498", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Body, Sexuality, Family, and Authority", "description": "This applied section asks how religious ethics shapes contested questions of embodiment, gender roles, sexuality, family, reproductive agency, and authority. Students practice analysis that is historically grounded and attentive to power.", "children": [{"id": "dbd7a89c-ed81-49a1-a5ff-2cbe4753e63a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, Power, and Interpretation", "children": [{"id": "aeb94a07-ae2f-4df2-88d1-df5e5aa58e97", "type": "concept", "title": "Patriarchy, matriarchy, complementarity, and equality"}, {"id": "bd1775df-ea9e-4e9a-ac9b-9fc5d3386efe", "type": "concept", "title": "Feminist, womanist, mujerista, and queer religious ethics"}, {"id": "5002757a-8b84-4fcf-a1c2-664ad7779d91", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority of women's and LGBTQ voices"}, {"id": "53a6b6f5-9e20-472d-931b-fcf21282b7f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretation of contested texts"}], "description": "Students learn how gender norms are justified, contested, and reinterpreted within religious traditions."}, {"id": "0d180810-ebf9-4ef1-8a35-57fa45cac0a4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sexuality, Marriage, Family, and Kinship", "children": [{"id": "490827fc-56e8-4533-be13-7f09d40b1c39", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage, divorce, celibacy, and singleness"}, {"id": "44f7af4b-3875-4859-96cf-e98dd530112e", "type": "concept", "title": "LGBTQ inclusion and exclusion"}, {"id": "7806696a-72a9-4136-93dc-0ff6070ea09f", "type": "concept", "title": "Parenthood, fertility, adoption, and kinship"}, {"id": "ab048752-7fbb-4e60-97f4-9b26237bb5b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent, abuse, and institutional accountability"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how traditions define legitimate intimacy, family obligation, celibacy, reproduction, and care."}, {"id": "4db63443-6f5c-4b52-8887-ef0ea7d30a43", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodiment, Disability, and Moral Agency", "children": [{"id": "be04340a-f196-4ae2-8f29-a8b99d4690e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Disability theology and ethics"}, {"id": "4d689f7c-7f67-4953-abc8-a0bd6c16bcdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Purity, modesty, health, and stigma"}, {"id": "bfc57dc9-ef58-4a69-88e4-fca75ded8806", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodied ritual practice"}, {"id": "86092b03-9533-46e4-9965-28c7db23a533", "type": "concept", "title": "Agency, dependence, and care"}], "description": "Students study bodies as sites of vulnerability, dignity, discipline, stigma, and divine or communal meaning."}]}, {"id": "4d9ac1d7-1b49-495f-a9b3-1e974f565447", "type": "topic", "title": "Violence, War, Peace, and Nonviolence", "description": "This section examines religious arguments about violence, protection, punishment, self-defense, peace, reconciliation, and repair. Students learn to distinguish ethical ideals from institutional and historical realities.", "children": [{"id": "5f3e0ba8-f281-43be-a3f1-2d35a818f7d4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Just War, Holy War, and Moral Limits", "children": [{"id": "c795bead-9b76-44b0-b6a9-7e9cf93dbe45", "type": "concept", "title": "Just cause, right authority, intention, proportionality, and discrimination"}, {"id": "4a4a38db-1cb4-4881-b07c-61c39b0d89b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Jihad, crusade, dharma-yuddha, and contested categories"}, {"id": "65e06193-7188-403f-8753-4de4d6c286b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Civilian immunity and moral injury"}, {"id": "f8ca6c0d-a89d-4938-9ab5-af38c1722431", "type": "concept", "title": "Terrorism, state violence, and religious legitimation"}], "description": "Students compare traditions that permit force under strict conditions and ask how moral limits are defined and enforced."}, {"id": "1b8fb269-c0b4-4c90-b628-0d725d4fdbe8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pacifism, Nonviolence, and Resistance", "children": [{"id": "89fd24f3-64cf-4756-a644-c040a36ecc3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Ahimsa, enemy love, and compassion"}, {"id": "4ead5a16-7fba-461b-a236-597eec79abd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscientious objection and civil disobedience"}, {"id": "b277049d-a8e3-460d-b9db-e23c744110d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Gandhi, King, Day, Thich Nhat Hanh, and other exemplars"}, {"id": "17d87421-b6ca-49c3-a0e5-aaee5ffafb45", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits and critiques of nonviolence"}], "description": "This subtopic studies nonviolence as discipline, strategy, witness, and spiritual practice."}, {"id": "b65a1f8e-c214-40f8-89f6-46850bb0558b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Peacebuilding, Reconciliation, and Restorative Justice", "children": [{"id": "3a7c71df-7956-4b8f-b5c3-45cf1ec7f8d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Trauma, memory, lament, and repentance"}, {"id": "0e40520e-a1f5-4a20-8096-bb7f63f1b4d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Restorative and transitional justice"}, {"id": "5759e82e-6b73-4518-9aa2-ce2aabd15823", "type": "concept", "title": "Interreligious peacebuilding"}, {"id": "69773bc4-af14-43eb-9fa5-3c8b8a9a8c39", "type": "concept", "title": "Reconciliation without impunity"}], "description": "Students examine religious resources for truth-telling, forgiveness, accountability, and repair after violence."}]}, {"id": "289ad3a2-ff68-4d72-8e6d-b5d4d6f6b002", "type": "topic", "title": "Economic Justice, Poverty, Work, and Global Inequality", "description": "Religious traditions offer rich but contested moral visions of property, labor, wealth, poverty, charity, debt, and justice. Students connect personal obligation with structural analysis.", "children": [{"id": "c262366e-9100-4ded-8318-2a668e08c0e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Wealth, Poverty, Charity, and Obligation", "children": [{"id": "a8a63630-c4b0-487c-9cab-53c3ffb46e30", "type": "concept", "title": "Almsgiving, tzedakah, zakat, dana, seva, and charity"}, {"id": "0fe45d08-630c-4af8-9413-3e5a0152703d", "type": "concept", "title": "Preferential concern for the poor"}, {"id": "f77b60fc-1ca5-4fd8-834e-27002d45df4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral danger of wealth and consumerism"}, {"id": "60c8e326-3223-45a4-839e-f4538b9974b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Paternalism, solidarity, and empowerment"}], "description": "Students study how traditions evaluate possession, generosity, greed, dependency, and duties to the poor."}, {"id": "5550572f-3441-4984-975a-95d1f176627e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor, Debt, Property, and Markets", "children": [{"id": "13f140c4-4efe-41d9-a660-b1d8833233ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Sabbath, rest, and humane labor"}, {"id": "d2c4e38e-e473-4307-80ea-e3e520b9adc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Usury, debt relief, and predatory lending"}, {"id": "dd0c65f4-8dff-4221-87e1-fe969e55eb8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Property, stewardship, and common goods"}, {"id": "8c36c15d-2544-4327-8814-65ce5c749f6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Fair trade, supply chains, and corporate accountability"}], "description": "This subtopic explores the ethical status of work, ownership, contracts, lending, profit, and exploitation."}, {"id": "322bf4a3-63a4-49f4-ba20-dc1ecbb1a018", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global Inequality and Development", "children": [{"id": "0377d096-ee68-46e3-abd9-eee2197a0fbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism, dependency, and global capitalism"}, {"id": "78ae0156-7282-45d2-bdbd-f35beb753943", "type": "concept", "title": "Migration, remittances, and labor mobility"}, {"id": "6e2fc213-a71a-491b-ae3e-42b07d8b5184", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanitarian aid and dignity"}, {"id": "a6701d16-02fa-46d6-993d-1ddc7875ecfc", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate injustice and economic responsibility"}], "description": "Students analyze poverty and inequality in global systems and ask what religious ethics contributes to development debates."}]}, {"id": "1e982d61-461a-4d86-8b07-e0c42ef81f75", "type": "topic", "title": "Environmental Ethics, Animals, and the More-than-Human World", "description": "This section asks how religious traditions imagine human beings in relation to land, water, animals, ecosystems, ancestors, and future generations. Students connect moral imagination to ecological practice.", "children": [{"id": "8bb0263b-3a52-435b-8206-1fee6a11fd85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Creation, Interdependence, Stewardship, and Kinship", "children": [{"id": "431e30cd-3248-4a02-8315-a568090ac517", "type": "concept", "title": "Creation care and stewardship"}, {"id": "b6c03c0a-938c-4af4-81b3-b3d677353139", "type": "concept", "title": "Dependent origination and ecological interbeing"}, {"id": "c06b3667-fc37-4acc-ac5b-ae739e30aebd", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous and place-based responsibilities"}, {"id": "22ba26aa-8873-463b-bd6c-c0df92ea9eb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacramentality, sacred place, and pilgrimage"}], "description": "Students compare models that view nature as creation, sacred order, interdependent process, kinship network, or moral community."}, {"id": "24d65ae0-6341-4ef7-9298-423d8136db05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Animals, Food, Sacrifice, and Compassion", "children": [{"id": "987c5cc2-32c1-4197-9031-fd309c5c2087", "type": "concept", "title": "Vegetarianism, fasting, and dietary law"}, {"id": "cd1194ed-12f5-4f13-96cb-35dfa7ad0f26", "type": "concept", "title": "Animal sacrifice and ritual meaning"}, {"id": "abc935ee-4d6c-425b-9b14-04f523d115fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Animal welfare, rights, and compassion"}, {"id": "54d9e5af-8634-499f-94e4-669953872ad0", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrial agriculture and ecological harm"}], "description": "This subtopic studies moral duties toward animals through food practices, sacrifice, hunting, companionship, labor, and non-harm."}, {"id": "4b85fd36-a5ae-48f3-b78c-b78aff614694", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Climate Ethics and Ecological Repair", "children": [{"id": "ced4e4e5-3bcf-455c-9853-b4fe85c61b4b", "type": "concept", "title": "Intergenerational justice"}, {"id": "cf3e16bb-c40e-4531-9d33-ec468066afe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental racism and climate vulnerability"}, {"id": "af6d5793-1a6f-46d3-8fb4-d5e7cd53d62d", "type": "concept", "title": "Simplicity, restraint, and conversion of desire"}, {"id": "74c2b10c-0ec9-4467-aa9a-3e170225a7c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Activism, policy, and institutional divestment"}], "description": "Students apply religious ethical frameworks to climate change, extraction, consumption, and repair."}]}, {"id": "9eecd585-5ee2-4a05-a75b-cd9bb3e748fd", "type": "topic", "title": "Bioethics, Technology, and Human Dignity", "description": "This section connects religious ethics with medicine and emerging technologies. Students learn to analyze dignity, suffering, autonomy, dependency, embodiment, mortality, and the limits of human control.", "children": [{"id": "788d6759-7945-453e-bbfb-a8c4cb33ff3b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Beginning of Life, Reproduction, and Family Formation", "children": [{"id": "2e36d86d-06d3-4ae1-8fcf-f1261c3d799f", "type": "concept", "title": "Abortion and moral status debates"}, {"id": "45bee3ed-d5b5-46c3-9381-725aad8ca036", "type": "concept", "title": "IVF, surrogacy, genetic selection, and adoption"}, {"id": "925dc25b-54ef-4d74-bbf8-d980b9625dad", "type": "concept", "title": "Maternal health and reproductive justice"}, {"id": "7373249b-bd4c-4dfd-a0e4-e7241ce0ed29", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscience, law, and pluralistic policy"}], "description": "Students compare religious moral reasoning about fertility, pregnancy, reproductive technologies, parenthood, and the status of prenatal life."}, {"id": "c1bfc15a-8795-4fb2-ba0b-9348aabed41a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "End of Life, Suffering, and Care", "children": [{"id": "ca5f9556-711b-495f-ac89-9e3e570ccd80", "type": "concept", "title": "Euthanasia and assisted dying"}, {"id": "c9dff6b1-8b60-4fe5-bae0-7b81e0e17541", "type": "concept", "title": "Palliative care and hospice"}, {"id": "4024e9bf-89ce-4c2f-8951-568065408cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Ordinary and extraordinary treatment"}, {"id": "5cafba4a-68f7-4193-af42-5b0374da72b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, grief, and communal support"}], "description": "This subtopic examines mortality, suffering, hope, autonomy, and care at the end of life."}, {"id": "01f383aa-ba51-493d-a4de-bc7f2e33ace3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emerging Technology, AI, and Human Dignity", "children": [{"id": "5c2fe156-0e47-49d3-b9af-b66e6807b213", "type": "concept", "title": "Genomics, cloning, enhancement, and disability critique"}, {"id": "ca453a6f-5123-4656-bb39-e471cbcbea80", "type": "concept", "title": "AI, automation, and moral agency"}, {"id": "5dfd778c-ac7a-40b1-839a-2451ad76152d", "type": "concept", "title": "Privacy, surveillance, and personhood"}, {"id": "70f0d884-b5c0-4f7d-9919-984863311324", "type": "concept", "title": "Technology as tool, idol, gift, and risk"}], "description": "Students extend religious moral frameworks to biotechnology, artificial intelligence, surveillance, enhancement, and data ethics."}]}, {"id": "276b2f3e-04f5-40ed-ae91-8e1d46ec4a24", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Law, Politics, Human Rights, and Public Life", "description": "Religious ethics often enters public life through law, advocacy, voting, protest, institutions, human rights, and civic argument. Students learn how religious reasons can contribute to pluralistic public deliberation.", "children": [{"id": "422be614-b31c-441d-8700-5f254c17deb3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pluralism, Religious Freedom, and Conscience", "children": [{"id": "fdf78bed-8304-4910-9681-0bcd2aef67cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Free exercise, establishment, and public reason"}, {"id": "05ea64d4-f7f2-4446-b786-43fc8017be1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscience claims and institutional exemptions"}, {"id": "d3b4f323-3e49-448b-b24a-411786fd41c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority religions and equal citizenship"}, {"id": "e9560b5a-0c1a-4265-b01e-7f7cfd88d36d", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious literacy in civic life"}], "description": "Students examine the ethical and legal problems that arise when diverse communities disagree about ultimate goods."}, {"id": "76eb1d38-13e7-40e4-8dcb-25ed377d34fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Human Rights, Dignity, and Critique", "children": [{"id": "b32da8ec-624c-4e02-a4a0-86eec1060432", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal dignity and particular traditions"}, {"id": "17265e82-dd97-48ca-adcf-9283dd37a387", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights, duties, and community obligations"}, {"id": "9c5f0a4e-ebeb-4ee3-ac8a-5d99963822ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, sexuality, caste, race, and religious freedom conflicts"}, {"id": "18342a41-7470-48f0-b551-7a0bf176aee2", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanitarianism, advocacy, and power"}], "description": "This subtopic studies religious support for human rights as well as religious critiques of rights language."}, {"id": "90f3a16d-c3ce-4957-bcd4-95b4daf93208", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Democracy, Nationalism, and Public Theology", "children": [{"id": "aac04bbd-a34f-4ca7-83f1-6bab6de631de", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil religion and nationalism"}, {"id": "37475866-46c5-418e-b48e-fa4f27315162", "type": "concept", "title": "Prophetic critique and democratic accountability"}, {"id": "e3483b0a-a11d-4319-a46e-ccc40043f152", "type": "concept", "title": "Theocracy, secularism, and post-secular politics"}, {"id": "af00d418-1852-4053-a9dc-a873e382fae4", "type": "concept", "title": "Misinformation, polarization, and moral leadership"}], "description": "Students analyze how religious ethics can support, challenge, or be captured by political movements."}]}, {"id": "cd1de906-aaf2-4424-8fab-22da25b1c9c4", "type": "topic", "title": "Case Study Lab, Research Paper, and Ethical Deliberation Capstone", "description": "The capstone turns knowledge into performance. Students learn to conduct research, compare traditions, interpret sources, deliberate with peers, and write a clear ethical argument.", "children": [{"id": "7dd40559-1019-4321-ae76-9968f0eb3efb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case Analysis Method", "children": [{"id": "fd11071f-847a-4c9d-9b84-6799b337a867", "type": "concept", "title": "Facts, stakeholders, harms, goods, and constraints"}, {"id": "7a82cac1-3237-4075-8ac2-a825639e12bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Relevant sources and moral frameworks"}, {"id": "6b218e70-c68c-4e46-90ef-560734821823", "type": "concept", "title": "Alternative judgments and objections"}, {"id": "f975b443-d0b4-406c-ba07-42c586e096d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical recommendations and accountability"}], "description": "Students use a repeatable method to move from a messy problem to a disciplined ethical judgment."}, {"id": "cfdd93a4-df14-4a2d-91b0-6f9ab2ff7b37", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research Design and Source Use", "children": [{"id": "173ca8e8-fdd0-4c29-aedd-df6dbd624485", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary texts, commentaries, and ethnographic evidence"}, {"id": "9c8ed1f6-331e-4059-9a0c-32d68d0ef7bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer-reviewed scholarship and source evaluation"}, {"id": "893dc6da-b821-40cc-86bd-575ea06aba11", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative thesis construction"}, {"id": "defe806c-f02f-44de-aca6-6b803ac8b2d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation, academic integrity, and positionality"}], "description": "This subtopic trains students to build a credible paper from primary sources, scholarship, and applied cases."}, {"id": "42989f21-5da0-480d-80b0-f33cae36483f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Deliberation, Presentation, and Revision", "children": [{"id": "9c61706e-fee4-49f8-a564-8f7ca51d98e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral briefing and seminar discussion"}, {"id": "a7b76ffd-a50c-446d-b1cd-9624cff1deba", "type": "concept", "title": "Responding to critique"}, {"id": "341455e2-ed7b-4ee1-af21-2ef5e70b02ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision for clarity and fairness"}, {"id": "56d1836e-0888-4232-8138-4fbf7553b4b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflective account of learning and judgment"}], "description": "Students practice explaining moral arguments to audiences who may not share the same religious commitments."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$rethjson$::jsonb,
  true,
